Ladybird sends each WebContent process a descriptor per enabled
extension that carries the content-script source text itself. WebKit
does the same: WebExtensionContext::addInjectedContent() reads every
script with WebExtension::resourceStringForPath() in the UI process
and ships it inside a WebCore::UserScript through the user content
controller. Blink keeps the scripts in a shared memory region that
UserScriptSet maps in each renderer. Gecko instead loads a content
script in the content process through the moz-extension protocol and
caches it in ExtensionContent.sys.mjs’s ScriptCache. Reading in the
UI process keeps the extension directory out of the WebContent
sandbox, which can’t read arbitrary files.
